            "text": "There is a particular kind of magic in a city before the shops open. The streets have not yet decided what sort of day they will have. Delivery trucks murmur at the curbs, pigeons conduct urgent meetings, and someone in an upstairs kitchen drops a spoon into the waiting sink.\n\nAt this hour, ordinary things seem briefly unassigned. A bench is not street furniture but a place where a life might change. A closed bakery holds the promise of warmth. Even the traffic lights look patient, offering their colors to an empty crossing as though practicing kindness without an audience.\n\nI like the thought that the world contains these small rehearsals. The kettle begins its song before anyone listens. Trees make shade before a traveler arrives. Somewhere, a person sets out two cups, although the guest has not knocked. Hope often looks less like certainty than like preparing a chair.\n\nOf course, morning does not solve everything. Bills remain on tables. Grief wakes when we do. The news finds its way beneath the door. But there is something quietly defiant about buttering toast, watering a plant, or opening a window anyway: a refusal to let difficulty become the only story.\n\nSoon the shutters rise. Footsteps multiply. Someone misses a bus and someone else catches one by a miracle of timing. The city becomes itself again, noisy and unfinished. Yet that earlier stillness remains available, tucked behind the day like a note in a pocket: begin gently; you are still becoming."
